="#path_RatingStar_undefined-2small" class="RatingStar__backgroundFill"></use><path class="RatingStar__fill"></path></svg></span></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/8555063087">April 27, 2026</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted">Not so much slow burn as undercooked in this novel where a Texan cleaner wins the heart of local princess with barbecue chicken and an empathy with horses. <br /><br />It’s an interesting world build where people are assigned a caste based on the their magical powers, with at the peak, a Texan princess having the power to bring in hurricanes. The main characters are ordinary people (if somewhat obsessed by horses) who try to do the decent thing.<br /><br />It should have been a wonderful world but it annoyed me: <br />1) sexism - the princess has been brought up politics and has the power to bring in storms, but she’s treated like an idiot girl whereas a man with only life experience is treated as knowledgeable <br />2) 2d - with the exception of the MMC none of the other characters have any personality<br />3) it’s really sssslllllllllllooooooooowwwwwww<br />4) the epilogue - not the most hope filling epilogue out there.<br /><br />But hey, lots of other people have given it 4 or 5 star ratings, so what do I know?</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Review unliked. Press to like review"><span class="Button_